{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And all the congregation lifted up their voice and gave forth their voice.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): they gave forth their voice in a great weeping at eventide, the end of the day on which the spies came to them, for they came in the evening and brought darkness upon the world, and this is what is written: ״ויבכו העם בלילה ההוא״ — and the people wept on that night — which is what is written (Lamentations 1): ״בכה תבכה בלילה״, and it was the night of the ninth of Av. How so? Thus said our Rabbis of blessed memory: In the first year Moses made the Tabernacle; in the second he erected it and sent the spies, as it is written (Numbers 10): ״ויהי בשנה השנית בחדש השני בעשרים בחדש נעלה הענן״ — and the cloud ascended on the twentieth of the month. There remained nine days of Iyyar, which is always defective, and it is written (ibid.): ״ויסעו מהר ה׳ דרך שלשת ימים״, and the first day of those three we do not count, since it is the twentieth day of the cloud's ascension. And Rabbi Ḥama son of Rabbi Ḥanina said: on that day they turned away from after HaShem, as it is written (ibid. 11): ״והאספסוף אשר בקרבו התאוו תאוה וישובו ויבכו גם בני ישראל״ — hence it was the twenty-second of Iyyar. And it is written (ibid.): ״עד חדש ימים״ — that makes the twenty-second of Sivan. And it is written (ibid. 12): ״ותסגר מרים שבעת ימים״ — that makes the twenty-ninth of Sivan. And it was taught: on the twenty-ninth of Sivan Moses sent the spies. And it is written: ״וישובו מתור הארץ מקץ ארבעים יום״ — they concluded on the ninth of Av: the thirtieth of Sivan, which is full, and the twenty-ninth of Tammuz, which is always defective, makes thirty, and nine of Av makes thirty-nine. Therefore they raised the difficulty in the Gemara: if so, one day is missing from the forty. And the answer given: Tammuz of that year they made full — that is, Tammuz of the year of the spies was full, as it is written (Lamentations 1): ״קרא עלי מועד לשבור בחורי״ — He appointed a fixed time for the breaking of my young men on the ninth of Av. And it is written: ״ותשא כל העדה ויתנו את קולם ויבכו״. Rabbi Yoḥanan said: that day was the ninth of Av. The Holy One, blessed be He, said: You wept a baseless weeping — I shall establish it for you as a weeping for all generations, for on it the Temple was destroyed, both the first and the second. And therefore the prophet doubled the expression ״בכה תבכה״, and our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: בכה — in the first destruction; תבכה — in the second destruction. And further they interpreted it: בכה — over the kingdom of Israel; תבכה — over the kingdom of the house of Judah; בכה — over Jerusalem; תבכה — over Zion; בכה — over the ten tribes; תבכה — over Judah and Benjamin. And we find that the destruction of the first Temple was at night, as it is explicitly written (Jeremiah 39): ״ויהי כאשר ראם צדקיהו מלך יהודה וכל אנשי המלחמה ויברחו ויצאו לילה מן העיר דרך גן המלך״, and likewise Jeremiah prophesied further and said (ibid. 6): ״קומו ונעלה בלילה״. The destruction of the second Temple was also at night, as it is written (ibid.): ״אוי נא לנו כי פנה היום כי ינטו צללי ערב״. And when Titus the wicked came and destroyed Jerusalem and set fire within it, Israel went out and were exiled from Jerusalem and came to the camp of Titus; and there were Ishmaelite and Arab children there, and they took four hundred boys and girls and put them to sea at night and carried them off into captivity to their land. And the children said: Let us cast ourselves into the sea. They said to one another: We are afraid lest we have no share in the World to Come, and they would answer one another: But is it not written (Psalms 68): ״אמר ה׳ מבשן אשיב אשיב ממצולות ים״? When the girls heard this, they cast themselves in. The boys drew an a fortiori inference on their own: if these, whose way is thus, did so — how much the more so we! And all these events that befell Israel were at night, in punishment for the weeping they performed on this night. And further we find that on this night the decree of destruction and dispersion for Israel in exile was sealed, as it is written (ibid. 106): ״וירגנו באהליהם לא שמעו בקול ה׳ וישא ידו להם להפיל אותם במדבר ולהפיל זרעם בגוים ולזרותם בארצות״, and likewise it is written in Ezekiel (20): ״גם אני נשאתי את ידי להם במדבר להפיץ אותם בגוים ולזרות אותם בארצות״. Here you have it explicit from the Torah and from the Prophets and from the Writings that this weeping was established for all generations, that they would be scattered among the nations." "<b>By way of the Midrash.</b> ״ותשא כל העדה ויתנו את קולם״ — this is what the verse says (Jeremiah 12:8): ״נתנה עלי בקולה על כן שנאתיה״ — that voice by which you wept caused you to be punished. And concerning that generation Isaiah of blessed memory said (Isaiah 17:11): ״ביום נטעך תשגשגי ובבקר זרעך תפריחי נד קציר ביום נחלה וכאב אנוש״ — before the heat came you blossomed; נד קציר ביום נחלה — on the day that I passed over to give you the inheritance of your fathers, you became a rumor in the world; וכאב אנוש — the calamity you received became an inheritance for all generations, for the entire congregation wept on the night of the ninth of Av. The Holy One, blessed be He, said: You wept a baseless weeping — I shall establish for you a weeping for all generations. And from that hour the decree was sealed upon the Holy Temple that it be destroyed, as He says: ״וישא ידו להם להפיל אותם במדבר״ — the lifting of the hand corresponding to the lifting of the voice. And to this lifting of the voice Jeremiah alluded, saying (Jeremiah 11:16): ״זית רענן יפה פרי תאר קרא ה׳ שמך לקול המולה גדולה הצית אש עליה״ — that is, on account of the sin of the loud voice that Israel raised in the wilderness in weeping, the Holy One, blessed be He, set fire upon the owners of the olive tree mentioned at the beginning of the verse." "<b>And by way of the Kabbalah.</b> ״ותשא כל העדה״ — this is the Congregation of Israel, of whom it is written (Hosea 14:3): ״כל תשא עון וקח טוב״. And for this reason it does not say ״וישאו״ — and they lifted — but rather the singular ״ותשא״." "<b>And likewise</b> the masters of truth alluded in their midrash in Eikhah Rabbati: ״בכה תבכה בלילה״ — they read it as בכה תְבַכֶה. That is, it is an expression of causing to go out: He wept and caused the Congregation of Israel to weep with Him, as it is said: ״ותשא כל העדה״. And the allusion of the verse will be: ״ותשא כל העדה״ — the Congregation of Israel, which is ״כל העדה״; ״ויתנו את קולם״ — the ministering angels; ״ויבכו העם״ — these are Israel; ״בלילה ההוא״ — that is, all this was decreed on that night."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:32:31.965684614Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}